begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{4})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3+4+3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-127260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-127260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-127260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-127260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of heads after $4$ flips of an unfair coin follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the total number of heads after $4$ flips of the coin? Do not round the answer.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{21}$ | $\\frac{1}{21}$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{3}{7})+1(\\frac{2}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+3(\\frac{2}{21})+4(\\frac{1}{21}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+6+6+4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337183" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337183" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{22}{21}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A spinner has $1$ section labeled with two, $2$ sections labeled with four, $3$ sections labeled with six, and $4$ sections labeled with eight. If the spinner is spun once,  what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value shown on a randomly spun spinner. Then the support is $S={2,4,6,8}.$\n\nOn the spinner there are $1$ section labeled with two, $2$ sections labeled with four, $3$ sections labeled with six, and $4$ sections labeled with eight (ten sections in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(6) & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(8) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+4\\cdot f(4)+6\\cdot f(6)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{10})+4(\\frac{1}{5})+6(\\frac{3}{10})+8(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{8}{10}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+18+32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{10} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337206" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337206" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-83641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Several egg cartons were checked for cracked eggs. The number of cracked eggs in each carton was recorded below. If $N$ is the number of cracked eggs in a carton, what is the expected value of $N?$\n\n| Number of Cartons | $10$ | $7$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cracked Eggs | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-83641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the cracked eggs, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of cartons is $10+7+2+1=20,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(N=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(N=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{7}{20} \\\\ P(N=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{2}{20}=\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(N=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[N] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{2})+1(\\frac{7}{20})+2(\\frac{1}{10})+3(\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{4}{20}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+4+3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10} \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-83641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-83641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-83641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.4"] ["math" "0.7"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "1.4"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-83641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A production analyst records the number of flawed units in sampled batches. The data are summarized below. If $X$ is the number of flawed units in a batch, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Batches | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Flawed Units | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the flawed units, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of batches is $10+9+6=25,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{25} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{9}{25} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{10}{25}$ | $\\frac{9}{25}$ | $\\frac{6}{25}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{10}{25})+1(\\frac{9}{25})+2(\\frac{6}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337197" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337197" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.84"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A technician reviews inspection logs for defective components. The number of defects found per inspection is summarized below. If $X$ is the number of defective components found in an inspection, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Inspections | $5$ | $7$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Defects | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the defects, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of inspections is $5+7+4+3+1=20,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{7}{20} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{4}{20}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{5}{20})+1(\\frac{7}{20})+2(\\frac{4}{20})+3(\\frac{3}{20})+4(\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{8}{20}+\\frac{9}{20}+\\frac{4}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337195" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337195" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A jar has $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve. If one chip is randomly drawn from the jar,  what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn chip. Then the support is $S={1,4,9,12}.$\n\nIn the jar, there are $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve (fifteen chips in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =\\frac{6}{15}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(9) & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(12) & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(4)+9\\cdot f(9)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+4(\\frac{4}{15})+9(\\frac{1}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{27}{15}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+16+27+24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337208" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337208" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{73}{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following game featuring an unfair six-sided die. The probability that the die shows an odd number is $\\frac{2}{3},$ and the probability that the die shows an even number is $\\frac{1}{3}.$ If the roll of a die gives an odd number, the player loses $$4.$ If the roll of a die gives an even number, the player wins $$11.$ What is the expected profit of each roll in this game?\n\n$\\text{E}[p]=$${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$, $4,$ or $6$), the player wins $$11,$ so we have $p=11.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-4)=\\frac{2}{3},\\,P(11)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-4$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-4)\\cdot f(-4)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =(-4)(\\frac{2}{3})+11(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$1.00.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337334" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337334" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-276301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following game featuring a fair five-sided die. If the roll of a die gives an odd number, the player loses $$2.$ If the roll of a die gives an even number, the player wins $$10.$ What is the expected profit of each roll in this game?\n\n$\\text{E}[p]=$${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-276301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$ or $4$), the player wins $$10,$ so we have $p=10.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-2)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(10)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =(-2)(\\frac{3}{5})+10(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.80.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-276301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-276301" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-276301" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we play a game that costs $$2$ per round. Each player randomly selects one card from a standard $52$-card deck. If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. What is the expected profit of a player in this game?\n\nRounded to two decimal places, the expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p$ represent the profit in this game. There are $52$ possible cards that can be selected. One of these cards is the winning card. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ This means the player’s profit is $$100-$2=$98,$ so we have $p=98.$\n\n- If the ace of spades is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(98)=\\frac{1}{52},\\,P(-2)=\\frac{51}{52}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $98$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{52}$ | $\\frac{51}{52}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =98\\cdot f(98)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =98(\\frac{1}{52})-2(\\frac{51}{52}) \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{52}-\\frac{102}{52} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is approximately $-$\\,0.08.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337338" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337338" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.08"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2.$ The outcomes of the coin have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{1}{4},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8,$ so we have $p=8.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $8$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =8\\cdot f(8)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+(-2)(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4}-\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$0.50.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337335" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337335" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4.$ The coin outcomes have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{7}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $5$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =5\\cdot f(5)+(-4)\\cdot f(-4) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{10})+(-4)(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{28}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-28}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =-1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.30.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d95ca7eb57a9deb13e16e5a792946be2310d3c39188a2b4a46800aec741316e"} {"question" "q-337336" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "q-337336" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/state.json" "file_sha256" "5597f5ce6f52760eff9a0e8a49fd044fbd329e7cf877ff9ed676376aa22ba8fb"} {"question" "e-6074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the expected value of one roll of a tetrahedral die?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-6074.json" "file_sha256" "88b6a3172a604c9085926b43be7aea4bc7403046613ea25e09e4e64e050b0bda"} {"question" "e-6074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nA tetrahedral die has four sides. Let $X$ be the score attained on a random roll of the die. Then the support is $S = \\{ 1,2,3,4 \\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\dfrac{1}{4}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1) = f(2) = f(3) = f(4) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-6074.json" "file_sha256" "88b6a3172a604c9085926b43be7aea4bc7403046613ea25e09e4e64e050b0bda"} {"question" "e-7383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rolls of an unfair tetrahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-7383.json" "file_sha256" "30f3de1ec69dfd54fc028cedd19f4b981e0b65847f57475afc10d8ecc2238d6b"} {"question" "e-7383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\frac{3}{10})+3(\\frac{2}{5})+4(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{12}{10}+\\frac{8}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+6+12+8}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{10} \\\\  & =2.7\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-7383.json" "file_sha256" "30f3de1ec69dfd54fc028cedd19f4b981e0b65847f57475afc10d8ecc2238d6b"} {"question" "e-7385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Several children were asked about how many pets they have. The results were recorded below. If $X$ is the number of pets that a randomly selected child has, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of pets | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $5$ | $6$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-7385.json" "file_sha256" "43a8dbafbb4a7f221bc82f9655973c8f631709d9085a022203ec1f6ca26aeec0"} {"question" "e-7385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of pets, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $5+6+5=16,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{16}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{5}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{5}{16})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{5}{16}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{16}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-7385.json" "file_sha256" "43a8dbafbb4a7f221bc82f9655973c8f631709d9085a022203ec1f6ca26aeec0"} {"question" "e-6075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that we play a game with a stack of five cards labeled $1$ through $5.$ A card is drawn at random. Drawing $1,2,3,$ or $4$ means that you lose $\\[math]1,$ and drawing a $5$ means that you win $\\[/math] 5.$ What is the expected profit at each drawing in this game?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-6075.json" "file_sha256" "fb4163a87862912c9e81fd77c4b7cfeccbb1bb8322549e0a75060c0aec5eb7ee"} {"question" "e-6075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\[math]1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\[/math]5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5)  \\[5pt]\n&= -1 \\left( \\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left( \\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\[3pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\[3pt]\n\t&=0.2\n\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\[math]0.20$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071101/example-6075.json" "file_sha256" "fb4163a87862912c9e81fd77c4b7cfeccbb1bb8322549e0a75060c0aec5eb7ee"}] "usage" {"e-7385" [] "e-6075" [] "e-6074" [] "e-7383" []} "basis" 2759 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
